course: dictionary
lesson: 9192
title: Socket: a hole where something fits
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A socket is a hollow place where something else fits or plugs in. Read the words around it to find out which kind of socket is meant. «When something doesn't work, test the simple causes first: is the socket switched on, is the cable in?» Here the socket is in a wall or a device, and you push a plug into it to get electricity.
explain: The same word can also mean a place in your body. «A squash ball is about 40 mm across, small enough to fit into the eye socket.» Your eye sits in a hollow in your skull, and that hollow is an eye socket. Ask yourself: what fits into this hole? A plug, or a part of the body?
explain: Look for clues such as plug, wall, eye or hip. «Swing your arm in a full circle: only the shoulder can do that, because it is a ball sitting in a shallow socket.» Here the socket is the hollow where the ball at the top of your arm sits, so your arm can turn in many directions. Put your meaning into the sentence to check that it makes sense.
fact: A socket is a hole in a wall or device where you push in a plug to get electricity.
fact: In the UK, Ireland, Australia and New Zealand it is often called a power point or plug socket; in the USA and Canada it is called an outlet.
fact: A socket is also a hollow place in the body where another part fits, like your eye socket or hip socket.
fact: Never put fingers or metal objects into a socket, and tell an adult at once about a damaged or sparking one.
fact: Ball-and-socket joints in your shoulder and hip let your arms and legs move in many directions.
exercise: «When something doesn't work, test the simple causes first: is the socket switched on, is the cable in?» What is a socket in this sentence? | *A hole in a wall or device where a plug goes | A small lamp that flickers | A type of sock | A bowl for washing your face
exercise: «A squash ball is about 40 mm across, small enough to fit into the eye socket.» Where is the eye socket? | In your pocket | *In the bone around your eye | In the front of a car | On the squash court floor
exercise: «Dinosaurs had legs straight under the body, which shows as a hole in the hip socket.» Which part of the body has the socket in this sentence? | legs | body | *hip | dinosaurs
test: «Never put fingers or metal objects into a socket.» Why is this a safety rule? | Because sockets like to be kept clean | Because metal is too heavy to lift | *Because it can give you an electric shock | Because it makes the plug shine
test: «Ball-and-socket joints in the shoulder and hip let arms and legs move in many directions.» What do these joints let arms and legs do? | *Move in many directions | Grow longer each year | Only move up and down | Stay completely still
exam: In the UK a socket on the wall is often called a power point, but in the USA and Canada it is called what? | *An outlet | A plug | A cable | A switch
